  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front tracked across the BGM forecast area earlier this \nmorning with a few showers along the boundary. Breaks in the\nclouds at times and a few showers will be possible with a\nnorthwest flow off the lower Great Lakes. 850 mb temperatures \nare falling quickly behind the front with surface temperatures \ngradually falling through the evening. \n\nWith a trough crossing the region and temperatures aloft\ncooling, the 850 mb temperatures get cold enough for some lake \neffect precipitation to form. Initially, it will be warm enough \nthis evening for rain or a rain snow mix, but tonight, \nprecipitation will change to snow. The vertical wind profile is \nnot favorable for any organized banding off the Great Lakes so \nthe lake effect snow will likely be more scattered snow showers.\nSnow accumulations are not expected for most of the area though\na few higher elevations in the Finger Lakes and CNY may get a \ndusting to an inch on elevated surfaces. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModels continue to be in good agreement with the late week low \nforming off the Southeast Coast then moving north up through the\nAppalachian Mountains into the Northeast. The low is developing\nat the nose of a subtropical jet streak with a neutral to \nslightly negatively tilted trough in the central US. The trough \ndoes not contain a lot of cold air. Deep, subtropical moisture \nwraps into the the developing low on the east and northeast \nside. This warm air will be advecting into the Northeast well \nahead of the low, with SE 850 mb flow developing by Wednesday \nnight. 850 mb temperatures may make a run for around 10C by the \ntime the precipitation begins to arrive so the potential for any\nmixed precipitation at onset is gone so rain is the likely \nprecipitation type for this entire storm. \n\nHigher winds have potential with a strong low level jet moving \nin on the North side of the low as the precipitation moves in. \nForecast soundings have winds at 3000 feet above the surface \napproaching 60 to 70 mph. Forecast soundings are very stable in \nthe low levels so the ability to transfer some of that momentum \ndown is greatly limited. BUFKIT soundings continue to only \nindicate wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph reaching the surface through\nthe stable layer so odds are we stay largely below wind \nadvisory criteria but will be something to watch. The peaks of \nthe Catskills may see some of those higher winds though. \n\nAs the low passes through New York Friday into Saturday, it \nbegins to occlude and becomes stacked. The rain will become more\nshowery in nature with winds decreasing significantly. Model \ntrends continue with less cold air wrapping into the occluding \nlow from the Great Lakes trough so even after cold air advection\nbegins, precipitation likely does not change over to snow. The \nexception may be higher elevations in Steuben or the Catskills \nabove 2000 feet.",
